;;; fuel-exhausted.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: fuel-exhausted   ·   family: language (sandbox limit)   ·   counted-real

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — fuel-exhausted")

(error-card :key "fuel-exhausted"
  :name "Fuel exhausted"
  :family "language"
  :census-status "counted-real"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/fuel-exhausted.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"Not a classical R7RS error. It is Motoi's own bounded-evaluation guard: every
run carries a FUEL BUDGET (a step counter). When evaluation takes more steps than
the budget allows, the interpreter stops itself and throws `fuel exhausted`
rather than hanging. It is the language refusing to loop forever."

  :trigger-code "(define (loop n) (loop (+ n 1))) (loop 0)"
  :observed-output "error: fuel exhausted"
  :normalizes-to "fuel-exhausted"

  :meaning
"The program consumed its entire step budget before producing a value. In almost
every case that means a NON-TERMINATING computation: an unbounded recursion with
no base case reached, an accidental infinite loop, or a genuinely huge amount of
work attempted in one run. Motoi caps steps so a runaway program fails loudly and
quickly instead of freezing the console.

It is a clean, thrown error that escapes to the boundary, so the census records
it under `fuel-exhausted`. A steady trickle of these is normal during
development (you write the recursion, forget the base case, get told). A SPIKE
across a session usually means one specific shape is looping."

  :common-causes (
    "a recursion whose base case is never reached — `(loop (+ n 1))` with no stop"
    "a base case that is written but never TRUE — `(if (= n -1) ...)` counting up"
    "mutual recursion where neither branch shrinks the problem"
    "an unbounded `let`/named-let iteration with a wrong or missing terminator"
    "genuinely large work in one run — sometimes the fix is to do less, or to
     stream, not to raise the ceiling")

  :at-the-verb
"Fuel is not charged to one verb; it is charged to the whole evaluation. But the
CALL COUNTERS tell you where the fuel went: after a `fuel exhausted`, look at
`(stats/verb-calls 5)` — the verb with a runaway count is the hot spot of the
loop. This is the census and the fuel guard working together: the error says
'something looped', the counters say 'and it was mostly THIS'."

  :how-to-detect
"You CANNOT catch fuel exhaustion with `guard`. Once the budget is spent, there
is no fuel left to run the handler, so the error escapes THROUGH the guard to the
boundary. This is unlike an ordinary error, which `guard` catches cleanly. The
difference is itself the lesson: a `guard` protects against errors that leave
budget behind; it cannot protect against running OUT of budget. Verify:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(display (guard (e (#t 'caught-fuel)) (let loop ((n 0)) (loop (+ n 1)))))"
  :how-to-detect-output "error: fuel exhausted"
  :how-to-detect-note
"Contrast with an ordinary error, which the SAME guard shape DOES catch:
    (display (guard (e (#t (list 'caught (error-object-message e)))) (error \"boom\")))
    => (caught boom)
So the detection for fuel is PREVENTIVE, not reactive: bound the WORK yourself
(carry an explicit depth counter and stop at it) rather than trying to catch the
exhaustion after the fact."

  :fix-shape
"Because you cannot catch it, you must PREVENT it. Find the recursion that does
not shrink toward a base case, and give it one that is actually reached. Confirm
the terminator's predicate can become true for the direction the argument moves
(counting UP toward a DOWN base case never terminates). If the work is
legitimately large, restructure it — fold instead of re-walk, memoize, or split
the run — or carry your own depth cap and return a partial result before the fuel
guard fires. Never just ask for a bigger budget for a loop that does not end."

  :lesson
"Fuel exhaustion is Motoi keeping a promise the halting problem will not let it
keep in general: it cannot prove your loop ends, so it bounds the loop and tells
you when the bound is hit. Read it as 'this did not terminate in the budget', and
let the call counters point you at the culprit. The guard is a feature — it turns
the worst failure mode of a Lisp (a frozen REPL) into a fast, named, countable
error."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-count-of \"fuel-exhausted\")"
    "(stats/verb-calls 5)")

  :related-broken-examples ("surreal-simplest-oom.slatl")
  :related-cards ("macro-fuel-exhausted" "roster-not-observed"))
